[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one lands on rung 1 of the ladder — session lifetime is security posture, so I want your confirmation before
  editing, even though it's a one-line change.

  [1mThe change:[0m [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m — [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m a stolen or abandoned session stays valid for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. That's a 16x wider
  window for session hijacking — a token lifted from a shared machine, an unlocked laptop, an XSS-exfiltrated cookie, or
  a proxy log stays usable for a full working day. [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m reads the value directly, so nothing else caps it and
  there's no idle-vs-absolute distinction here: it's the only expiry control in the code.

  Confirm and I'll make the change as asked. If the goal is "stop logging people out mid-work," the usual alternative is
  a short idle timeout with sliding renewal plus a longer absolute cap — but that's a design change, not this edit, so
  say the word if you'd rather explore it.

